foreign  hi guys it's Quinn here if you enjoy my  videos consider hitting the like button  it's the only way the YouTube algorithm  really notices me  So today we're going to talk about the  culture series by Ian M Banks this is  another large sprawling science fiction  Universe with many of its own unique and  interesting science fiction elements so  first of all there is a lot of books in  this series they all follow different  characters throughout the Universe and  different circumstances and situations  so in this series the culture is a  post-scarcity society kind of like the  one in Star Trek where humans and  humanoid aliens along with sentient  artificial intelligence and sentient  machines  live in harmony and what is basically a  Utopia now they don't really inhabit  planets they usually are on ships or  what they call orbitals which are  basically space stations you can also  consider the culture to be kind of a  post-human society because people aren't  afraid to upgrade themselves they have  all sorts of mental enhancements  physical enhancements sexual  enhancements they've been altered so  they can live for hundreds of years turn  off pain at will they can induce the  effects of psychoactive drugs at will  and essentially have a psychedelic trip  whenever they want instantaneously the  culture is a society that holds freedom  and individuality in high regard it's  essentially the best case scenario as  far as the future of humanity could go  and also similar to Star Trek the main  themes of this series center around the  moral and ethical dilemmas that the  culture encounters when dealing with  other civilizations and other societies  throughout this series  the culture is led by Minds the minds  are the most intelligent beings  biological or otherwise in the culture  now essentially a mind acts as the  center of a ship or a space habitat it  has the Computing capability to run the  entire space station or ship some  planets actually even have mines they're  so powerful Computing wise because  they're actually four-dimensional  entities and much of their computing  power comes from the fact that they  exist partially in hyperspace and can  calculate at essentially light speed so  I mentioned that there were other  civilizations in this story now the  culture is considered to be a high level  involved  civilization also called an optimey  civilization  so optimey civilizations like the  culture and others like it were often  the mentors and sometimes the rulers of  other civilizations  and then they're also the lower level  involved civilizations which had  achieved you know essentially  galaxy-wide contact with other societies  there were a small number of involved  civilizations more than Optimist  civilizations of course but certainly  not as many as there were Interstellar  civilizations that weren't sufficiently  influential there were potentially  thousands of these there are also what  are referred to as Elder civilizations  which are the civilizations that did  reach the level of advancement that  would allow for them to go Interstellar  but kind of retreated and didn't really  become involved in any Intergalactic  meddling  but finally  one of the most interesting and honestly  kind of terrifying types of  civilizations brought up in the Ian  Banks books is the hegemonizing Swarm  so the hegemonizing Swarm is a  civilization  but not really a civilization a  collection of entities that exist only  to convert the universe into more of  itself and it will do this until it has  used up all of the energy and matter in  the entire universe now this goes back  to a concept that's been talked about  and written about for years in his 1986  book engines of creation nanotechnology  Pioneer K Eric Drexler coins the term  gray Goo now gray goo is a hypothetical  catastrophic scenario which involves  self-replicating machines typically  molecular nanotechnology which grows  beyond our ability to control it and in  order to continue replicating consumes  all of the biomass on Earth because all  it knows how to do its main function is  to build more and more of itself so in  his culture series Ian Banks adopts the  term gray goo to describe the  hegeminizing Swarm type civilization  now it wasn't just these types of  self-replicating machine-based societies  that were referred to as the geminizing  Swarms but it could also be any type of  society that was singularly focused on  conquest and control of other worlds in  fact one of the central dilemmas that  the culture encounters actually involves  them Towing the balance when it comes to  intervening with other worlds and other  civilizations the balance between being  helpful and potentially becoming a  hegeminizing swarm themselves something  that just keeps growing and keeps making  more things into the same thing that it  is  so there's the Dilemma of can the  culture become the very thing that it  itself fears the most and of course the  culture fears this  what civilization would actually desire  to become the thing that eats the  universe the geminizing Swarm is one of  the most interesting Concepts that gets  explored in this series when it comes to  a civilization that is only interested  in Endless expansion  the question of where is this all going  eventually has to be asked what I like  about this series is that the World  building is very rich it's very unique  it has its own flavor the first book  consider fliebus actually focuses on a  character who is a shapeshifter named  horza we also learn in this first book  that there is a war between the culture  and this race of aliens called the  adirins and we get to follow horiza  who gets caught up in all of this and he  just has a really interesting  adventurous throughout that book and I  highly recommend it to anyone that wants  to get started in the series don't even  worry about reading the rest of it just  read that first book and if you like it  definitely continue on if you want to  learn more about the universe and to  uncover more secrets and to understand  more about the goings-on with the  culture and their interactions with the  other civilizations in the galaxy  so the final thing that I like about  this series is the humor there were  times that I laughed out loud I think  Ian Banks has a great sense of humor and  I'd like to see humor in sci-fi books  because a lot of sci-fi books especially  ones that I tend to read are kind of  dark and Grim but this one includes  humor while also broaching on some  pretty interesting and deep topics and I  think it's a really nice balance  thanks for watching guys let me know if  you want to hear more about the culture  Universe make sure you like And  subscribe for more Quinn's ideas peace  out  just a heads up for those of you that  may be interested my next graphic novel  the LIE behind the star is launching  February 2023 you can sign up now to get  on the email mailing list to get  notified as soon as it launches more  information on my website link in the  description thank you guys so much  [Music]  thank you  [Music]  foreign  [Music]

